# Gitleaks commit and PR gate

- Never commit secrets or personal data, including personal emails, home paths, private network details, or phone numbers. Keep personal data out of prompts and PR titles/descriptions/comments; use neutral placeholders.
- Before any commit, if `gitleaks version` or `semgrep --version` fails, run `bash .cursor/install-security.sh` (and `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`), then re-run the scan.
- First run `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. The cloud install script installs pinned Gitleaks and enables the hook. If Gitleaks is missing, run `bash .cursor/install-security.sh` on Linux. In each new shell, include the fallback directory with `export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"`.
- Before every commit and before opening or updating a PR, run `.githooks/pre-commit` (or `gitleaks git --pre-commit --staged --redact -v --config .gitleaks.toml`). Stage the intended changes first. A failed or incomplete scan blocks the commit or PR update; fix the finding and rerun.
- Never use `--no-verify`. Never edit `.gitleaks.toml` or `.githooks` to hide a finding.
- Use the GitHub noreply identity for both commit author and committer. For this repository's owner, use `241515594+sfls1397@users.noreply.github.com`. Confirm the identity before committing; never use a personal email.
- Before merging, run `python3 .security/local-gate.py PR_NUMBER` and confirm it recorded a `local-gate` PASS on the exact PR head, and that any package-contents scan passes. GitHub Actions are not the merge gate. Keep `.cursor/` and `AGENTS.md` out of npm package contents.

- The shared hook now also runs production npm audit on staged package-file changes and offline Semgrep on staged source bytes. Before opening or updating a PR run `python3 .security/scan.py all` as well. Install the pinned Semgrep via the cloud security setup; new/changed findings, expired exceptions and incomplete scans block the gate. Read `.security/FINDINGS.md` for documented existing findings and exact exceptions. Never broaden them to hide a finding.

## Complete repository privacy and language gates

The privacy gate also checks raw Git author/committer headers and commit messages. New commits require GitHub noreply; history permits only GitHub noreply and the exact public tool identities documented by metadata.py. Mailmap cannot conceal private identities. Commit-msg scans messages for secrets; pre-push runs metadata, full-history Gitleaks and full dependency/code checks. Before merging, run `python3 .security/local-gate.py PR_NUMBER` from the reviewed PR checkout: it runs every gate plus this repository's tests (`.security/local-gate.json`) on the exact head in a clean worktree and records the result on that SHA as the `local-gate` commit status and a PR comment. Then `python3 .security/merge-pr.py PR_NUMBER` requires that recorded PASS from the owner on the exact head (GitHub Actions are not the merge gate), reruns full local gates, and merges with a matching-head condition. Public repositories also require the `local-gate` status in branch protection; private branch protection depends on the account plan, so there the command is an enforced agent workflow, not a GitHub-side access restriction.

The code gate includes checksum-pinned ShellCheck at warning/error threshold for shell files, including extensionless wrappers, and Clang Static Analyzer for C/Objective-C helpers on macOS. On macOS the local gate (`.security/local-gate.py`) runs the Clang analysis for native helpers; Linux reports the platform handoff explicitly. No source language is silently claimed as analyzed by Semgrep. Add language-appropriate checks before adding a new runtime language. All scanners run locally and report locations/rule names without source snippets. Runtime downloads and separately installed language environments require their own dependency audit; npm audit of the parent manifest alone does not cover them.
